# Shell scripts must reach a Linux runner with LF endings and the executable bit set.
#
# CRLF is the failure that looks like nothing: bash reads the trailing CR as part of the
# interpreter path and reports "/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory", which
# names a file that plainly exists. Committing from Windows with core.autocrlf=true is enough to
# introduce it, so it is pinned here rather than left to each clone's config.
#
# The executable bit is tracked separately, in the index rather than here. A .gitattributes rule
# cannot set it, so every new script needs this once, from a Windows clone as much as anywhere:
#
#   git update-index --chmod=+x <script>
#
# Forgetting it fails only on Linux, and only when something tries to EXECUTE the file rather than
# read it -- so it survives every local test on Windows and surfaces as
# "./requirements/strands/install.sh: Permission denied" in CI. That is how it was found.
#
# Scripts that must carry the bit, checkable with `git ls-files -s anchor '*.sh'`
# (want 100755, not 100644):
#
#   anchor                            the launcher, which dispatches the verb
#   build.sh                          the build entry point
#   build-docker.sh                   builds and smoke-tests the container image
#   requirements/strands/install.sh   invoked by .github/workflows/build.yml
#
# CI invokes install.sh as ./requirements/strands/install.sh rather than `bash <path>` deliberately:
# `bash <path>` would work with or without the bit, which means a lost bit would go unnoticed until
# somebody tried to run it by hand. This way CI is what catches it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The launcher carries no extension, deliberately -- `anchor check ...` is the command -- so the
# rule above does not reach it and it needs its own.
anchor text eol=lf
